--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Why this is physical enumeration and not a device-tree artifact
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

slim_device_probe() calls the driver's probe, then slim_get_logical_addr().
Only if that succeeds does it call slim_device_update_status(UP), which is
what invokes the device_status callback. slim_get_logical_addr() ends up in
slim_device_alloc_laddr(), which -- because the NGD controller supplies a
->get_laddr op -- calls qcom_slim_ngd_get_laddr().

qcom_slim_ngd_get_laddr() sends SLIM_USR_MC_ADDR_QUERY to the manager (on
the ADSP) carrying the elemental address, and waits synchronously:

	if (!memcmp(rbuf, failed_ea, 6))
		return -ENXIO;
	*laddr = rbuf[6];

If the codec were not physically present the manager returns an all-zero
elemental address, get_laddr returns -ENXIO, slim_device_probe logs
"Failed to get logical address" and returns -EPROBE_DEFER, and
device_status(UP) is never reached.

device_up therefore required the part to answer an address query over the
bus. Logical address 0x00 is a genuinely assigned address (first device on
this bus), not a null read.

